<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl August von Hardenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prince Hardenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl August von Hardenberg was a Prussian statesman who served as state chancellor from 1810 until his death in 1822. Continuing the reforms begun by Stein after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s defeat in 1806, he introduced freedom of trade, emancipated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1812 and reformed taxation and the peasants' obligations. He represented Prussia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, where it gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-august-von-hardenberg.jpg" alt="Karl August von Hardenberg" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Thomas_Lawrence_(1769-1830)_-_Charles_Augustus,_Prince_Hardenberg_(1750-1822)_-_RCIN_404941_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hardenberg was the eldest of eight children of Christian Ludwig von Hardenberg, a Hanoverian colonel who later became field marshal and commander-in-chief of the Hanoverian army. He was born at Essenrode Manor near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, his maternal grandfather's estate. After studying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> he entered the Hanoverian civil service in 1770 and, on the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, travelled widely, studying the workings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> at Regensburg.</p><p>In 1774 he married Countess Christiane von Reventlow. Her affair with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> caused a scandal that forced him out of Hanoverian service. In 1782 he joined the service of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, where his zeal for reform made him unpopular with the orthodox clergy and the conservative estates. His wife's conduct again made his position untenable, and he divorced her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1792 Hardenberg was appointed administrator of the principalities of Ansbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, which had passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> after the last margrave abdicated. He reformed traditional anomalies there and worked to extend Prussian influence in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> he became Prussian envoy and, as plenipotentiary at Basel, signed the peace with the French Republic in 1795. Summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1797 by King Frederick William III, he took an important post in the cabinet, and on 14 April 1804 he succeeded Count Haugwitz as foreign minister.</p><p>He would have preferred an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s march of three corps through Ansbach changed the king's mind. After Austerlitz, the Treaty of Schönbrunn of 15 December 1805 gave Prussia Hanover in return for all its territories in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and Napoleon, who disliked Hardenberg, secured his retirement. The Treaty of Tilsit later required his dismissal again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Hardenberg was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> as chancellor on 6 June 1810, after Stein's forced retirement. He carried out Stein's reorganisation: the military system was reformed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> abolished, municipal institutions fostered, and the civil service opened to all classes. He signed the first Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1814 and was made a prince on 3 June 1814. He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s chief representative at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, though he failed to win the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p>His influence then fell behind that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>. Before the Carlsbad conference of 1819 he signed a convention with Metternich that the historian Heinrich von Treitschke judged a surrender of a voice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s internal affairs. Though he saw the need for constitutional reform, Hardenberg drifted with the tide against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, and the constitutions he drafted never appeared. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> on 26 November 1822.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl August von Hardenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1806 the reforms of Karl vom Stein and <b>Karl August von Hardenberg</b> created ministries, municipal self-government and a freer economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Essenrode, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.37,10.64&amp;year=1796" aria-label="Essenrode on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4789 356 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karl August von Hardenberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5239 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5239 626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.37,10.64&amp;year=1796">What was here in 1796? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
